🚽 Test case: a user-defined function call must run exactly once, whatever
🚽 syntactic position it appears in.
🚽
🚽 ast_accept()'s generic pre-visit used to execute user-defined calls, and
🚽 the statement's own visitor then evaluated the same node again for real.
🚽 So a declaration, an assignment, or a call buried in a right-hand
🚽 expression ran the body TWICE and kept the second result. Pure functions
🚽 were unaffected, which is why it survived; anything with a side effect
🚽 was not. Loading a resource loaded it twice.
🚽
🚽 The sharpest version is the last case here. A function that reports
🚽 whether it did something returns the SECOND attempt's answer -- the one
🚽 that found the work already done and declined -- so the side effect
🚽 happens exactly once while the report says it did not happen at all.
🚽 A void function whose `bussin` expression is discarded: the value-bearing
🚽 return arms never see it, so the void arm is the only thing that can run
🚽 it for its side effect.
skibidi void_ret(rizz *n) { bussin bump(n) + 0; }

🚽 The mirror hazard. ast_accept()'s pre-visit DOES evaluate an array access,
🚽 so the void arm must not evaluate this a second time.
skibidi void_arr(rizz *n) { rizz a[3] = {7, 8, 9}; bussin a[bump(n)]; }

🚽 Two fields on purpose: sizeof(Pt) is then 8, distinct from sizeof(rizz),
🚽 so the layout assertion below cannot be satisfied by the scalar answer.
gang Pt { rizz x; rizz y; };
gang Fl { chad f; };
gang Outer { gang Pt inner; };

🚽 A chad field, so the TYPE path is pinned and not just the value path.
🚽 Every static type query resolves a member access by walking storage, which
🚽 a call base has none of until it runs -- and those queries are forbidden
🚽 to run it. So they reported no type, and the variadic marshaller fell
🚽 through to STDROT_INT: this printed 3.000000 through %f. Declared types
🚽 answer it without evaluating anything.
gang Fl make_fl() { gang Fl q; q.f = 3.5; bussin q; }
gang Outer make_outer(rizz *n) { gang Outer o; o.inner.x = bump(n) + 6; bussin o; }

🚽 The field is bump()+6, so the VALUE distinguishes "the call ran" from
🚽 "the call ran and its struct survived long enough to read". Counting calls
🚽 alone hid a version of this that invoked handle_function_call(), whose
🚽 VAR_STRUCT case is discard-and-free: the count was right and every field
🚽 read produced 0.
gang Pt make_pt(rizz *n) { gang Pt p; p.x = bump(n) + 6; bussin p; }

🚽 A void `bussin` of a field read off a call, and of an ASSIGNMENT.
🚽 ast_accept()'s assignment visitor already performs the write, so the void
🚽 arm must not evaluate it again -- that is the third visitor exception,
🚽 alongside the increments and array access.
skibidi v_field(rizz *n) { bussin make_pt(n).x; }
skibidi v_assign(rizz *n) { rizz x = 0; bussin x = bump(n); }

🚽 A by-value struct SOURCE read off a call -- `make_outer(n).inner` where a
🚽 whole struct value is expected, not a scalar field. All three of these
🚽 keep the blob past the read: the argument binder copies into the
🚽 parameter later, in its bind loop; the return arm copies into a fresh
🚽 slot; the declaration copies into the variable's storage. But the call's
🚽 own blob lives in the shared pending-return slot, which the NEXT call
🚽 releases. So `take_pt(make_outer(&n).inner, bump(&n))` read freed memory
🚽 -- bump's cleanup freed the Outer, then the bind loop copied out of it --
🚽 and `bussin make_outer(n).inner;` leaked it, because that arm overwrites
🚽 the slot's pointer with its own fresh blob. The resolver hands back an
🚽 OWNED copy for this shape now; the scalar `f().x` reads above still
🚽 borrow, which is safe because they read before anything else runs.
skibidi take_pt(gang Pt v, rizz k) { yapping("field arg    %d %d", v.x, k); }
gang Pt pass_inner(rizz *n) { bussin make_outer(n).inner; }

rizz bump(rizz *n) {
    *n = *n + 1;
    bussin *n;
}

🚽 `rizz` rather than `cap` for the flag, because dereferencing a cap
🚽 pointer is rejected. And the parameter is `flag`, not `done`: if it
🚽 shares a name with the CALLER's local, `*flag` inside here resolves
🚽 against the caller's non-pointer variable and the call is rejected --
🚽 a separate scoping bug, avoided here rather than exercised.
cap try_once(rizz *flag) {
    edgy (*flag != 0) { bussin L; }
    *flag = 1;
    bussin W;
}

skibidi main {
    rizz calls = 0;
    rizz a = bump(&calls);
    yapping("declaration %d %d", calls, a);

    calls = 0;
    rizz b = 0;
    b = bump(&calls);
    yapping("assignment  %d %d", calls, b);

    calls = 0;
    rizz c = bump(&calls) + 0;
    yapping("expression  %d %d", calls, c);

    calls = 0;
    yapping("argument    %d", bump(&calls) * 0 + calls);

    calls = 0;
    bump(&calls);
    yapping("statement   %d", calls);

    calls = 0;
    edgy (bump(&calls) > 0) { }
    yapping("condition   %d", calls);

    rizz done = 0;
    cap fired = try_once(&done);
    yapping("reported    %b (must be W)", fired);

    🚽 Exactly once, not zero. These are the shapes where nothing on the
    🚽 ast_accept() path evaluates the expression, so making the pre-visit a
    🚽 no-op removed their ONLY evaluator rather than their second one. A
    🚽 bare call is special-cased elsewhere; a call inside any larger
    🚽 expression in statement position is not.
    calls = 0;
    bump(&calls) + 0;
    yapping("expr stmt   %d", calls);

    calls = 0;
    0 - bump(&calls);
    yapping("unary stmt  %d", calls);

    calls = 0;
    flex (rizz k = 0; k < 2; bump(&calls) + 0) { k = k + 1; }
    yapping("for-incr    %d", calls);

    calls = 0;
    void_ret(&calls);
    yapping("void bussin %d", calls);

    🚽 A field read off a call result, in every position that reaches the
    🚽 same owner. resolve_struct_access() executes the call itself, so a
    🚽 bare statement, a larger expression and a void `bussin` all work
    🚽 through one path rather than one special case each.
    🚽
    🚽 The nested form works too, and is pinned below -- it turned out to be
    🚽 the same defect rather than a separate one, since typing a chain reads
    🚽 the var_type the eager parse-time resolve fills in.
    calls = 0;
    make_pt(&calls).x;
    yapping("field stmt  %d", calls);

    calls = 0;
    rizz fv = make_pt(&calls).x;
    yapping("field decl  %d %d", calls, fv);

    calls = 0;
    rizz fa = 0;
    fa = make_pt(&calls).x;
    yapping("field assign %d %d", calls, fa);

    calls = 0;
    rizz fe = make_pt(&calls).x + 0;
    yapping("field expr  %d %d", calls, fe);

    calls = 0;
    cap fc = make_pt(&calls).x == 7;
    yapping("field cond  %d %b", calls, fc);

    calls = 0;
    yapping("field print %d", make_pt(&calls).x);

    calls = 0;
    v_field(&calls);
    yapping("field bussin %d", calls);

    🚽 The type path, not the value path: a chad field through %f, and
    🚽 maxxing(), whose operand is never evaluated so a static answer is the
    🚽 only kind allowed.
    yapping("field chad  %f", make_fl().f);
    yapping("field sizeof %d", maxxing(make_pt(&calls).x));

    🚽 Nested. Typing a chain reads the var_type filled in by the eager
    🚽 parse-time resolve, which cannot run a call -- so this needed the same
    🚽 static fallback rather than a fifth special case.
    calls = 0;
    yapping("field nested %d %d", make_outer(&calls).inner.x, calls);

    🚽 maxxing() of a struct-typed field off a call. Unlike the scalar case
    🚽 above this needs a LAYOUT, not a VarType, so it goes through a sixth
    🚽 site -- and once the type gate started passing, that site failed
    🚽 instead with "Invalid type in sizeof", printing 0. A scalar field
    🚽 cannot pin it, and neither can a one-field struct whose size happens
    🚽 to equal a rizz.
    yapping("field layout %d", maxxing(make_outer(&calls).inner));

    calls = 0;
    take_pt(make_outer(&calls).inner, bump(&calls));

    calls = 0;
    gang Pt pc = make_outer(&calls).inner;
    yapping("field copy   %d %d", calls, pc.x);

    calls = 0;
    gang Pt pr = pass_inner(&calls);
    yapping("field return %d %d", calls, pr.x);

    calls = 0;
    v_assign(&calls);
    yapping("void assign %d", calls);

    🚽 An array access inside a void `bussin`. The pre-visit already ran it,
    🚽 so the void arm must not.
    🚽
    🚽 This used to have to come last: a `bussin arr[f()]` in a void function
    🚽 followed by a struct-returning call segfaulted in
    🚽 free_pending_return_value(), reproducing on origin/main untouched.
    🚽 Blanking the pending slot's union fixed that, so the ordering here is
    🚽 no longer load-bearing.
    calls = 0;
    void_arr(&calls);
    yapping("void arr    %d", calls);

    🚽 The array mirror of the declaration/assignment double-eval. The
    🚽 pre-visit evaluates an array access, and the declaration's own visitor
    🚽 then evaluated the initializer again -- so the INDEX call ran twice and
    🚽 `arr[bump(&n) - 1]` read arr[1] instead of arr[0]. Fixing only the void
    🚽 `bussin` arm would have left this, which is why the declaration and
    🚽 assignment pre-visits consult the same predicate.
    calls = 0;
    rizz arr[3] = {7, 8, 9};
    rizz ai = arr[bump(&calls) - 1];
    yapping("array decl   %d %d", calls, ai);

    calls = 0;
    rizz aa = 0;
    aa = arr[bump(&calls) - 1];
    yapping("array assign %d %d", calls, aa);

    🚽 And the two the visitor DOES evaluate, which must not now run twice.
    rizz i = 0;
    i++;
    ++i;
    yapping("inc stmts   %d", i);

    bussin 0;
}
